  "configuration_id": "config_026",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (scgpt + imagenet_vit_l + avgpool + weighted, multiprofile) is the best at 0.5420 acc, and all completed experiments used scgpt as the RNA encoder. The only other RNA encoder tested (in rna_only config_036) was also scgpt, so we have zero evidence on whether scvi_n200 or pca_n2000 could match or exceed scgpt in the multimodal setting. Question: Does swapping scgpt for scvi_n200 in the best-performing multimodal configuration (imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted, multiprofile) maintain or improve accuracy? This directly tests the RNA encoder axis, which is the most important unexplored dimension given that all top results used scgpt. If scvi_n200 performs comparably, it likely trains cheaper (VAE vs. transformer-based encoder), improving the accuracy-per-GPU-hour ratio. If it underperforms, we confirm scgpt is the right choice and can focus remaining budget on other axes (e.g., single profile, resnet50 variants). Expected cost: moderate, roughly 2.0–2.5 GPU hours (scvi_n200 is lighter than scgpt, but imagenet_vit_l and multiprofile aggregation keep it in the same ballpark as config_001's 2.94 hrs). This is the best use of experiment 6 because it exploits the strongest configuration while reducing uncertainty on a high-impact design choice, leaving 4 experiments to exploit whichever RNA encoder wins."
}

